{% trace-scenario title="Fast to see it, or fast to know if it worked?" prompt="Your team just wired a CrewAI agent into a real workflow. You need to see what it did, wrap up debugging quickly, and — within a few weeks — start blocking bad deploys automatically and explaining per-request cost to finance. AgentOps gives you a replayable session in ten minutes. Uncoil gives you a session with an evaluation verdict and a dollar figure attached, in about twenty. Which one keeps you moving?" outcome="It depends on which question you're actually stuck on this month: 'what did the agent do' (AgentOps' strength) or 'was what it did good, and what did it cost' (Uncoil's strength). Most teams need both, just not on the same day." /%}

## At a glance

| Dimension | AgentOps | Uncoil |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Primary focus** | Auto-instrumented session recording and replay | Integrated tracing + evaluation + cost tracking |
| **Instrumentation model** | Decorator/wrapper auto-instrumentation, framework-aware | Native SDK, explicit spans, framework-aware |
| **Session replay** | Native, high-fidelity, action-by-action | Trace timeline view; replay is secondary to evaluation |
| **Evaluation framework** | Limited; mostly manual review plus newer add-on tooling | Integrated: test case library, LLM-as-judge, batch runner, regression alerts |
| **Cost tracking** | Basic token/spend visibility per session | Automatic per-call cost breakdown by model/provider, with rollups |
| **Framework integrations** | Deep, opinionated: CrewAI, AutoGen, LangChain, Python-first | Broad: LangChain, LlamaIndex, native OpenAI/Anthropic/Mistral, TS-first |
| **Setup time** | ~10 minutes to first recorded session | ~20-30 minutes to first evaluated, costed trace |
| **Deployment model** | SaaS (hosted dashboard) | SaaS (hosted dashboard) |
| **Best for** | Fast debugging of agent behavior during build-out | Production quality gates, cost accountability, experimentation |

AgentOps tells you exactly what your agent did. Uncoil tells you whether what it did was good, and what it cost you to find out.

### The operating model

**AgentOps** is built around *frictionless observation*: drop in a decorator or a couple of init calls, and every action, tool call, and LLM call inside an agent session gets recorded automatically, with framework-level awareness of CrewAI, AutoGen, and LangChain agent patterns. The dashboard's centerpiece is session replay — a step-by-step reconstruction of what the agent actually did, in order, with minimal instrumentation effort from you. **Uncoil** is built around *integrated production confidence*: the same category of tracing, plus a native evaluation framework, automatic cost calculation, and multi-turn session rollups, all attached to the same span data with no export-and-rebuild step. Choose AgentOps when your immediate bottleneck is understanding agent behavior with the least possible setup friction. Choose Uncoil when your bottleneck is proving the agent's outputs are good and knowing what they cost, on an ongoing basis.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Read this first" title="Both platforms trace agents well. The split is what happens after the trace lands." body="AgentOps and Uncoil both capture what your agent did — spans, tool calls, LLM calls, errors, in order. Where they diverge is the next step. AgentOps hands you a replay and stops there; judgment is yours, done by eye. Uncoil attaches a verdict (did this pass the test case, does this look like a regression) and a price (what did this cost, broken out by model and provider) to the same data, automatically. If your team's current constraint is 'we can't see what the agent did,' either platform solves it in under fifteen minutes. If your constraint is 'we can see it, but we can't tell if it's getting worse or what it's costing us,' only one of these two platforms was built to answer that." tone="paper" /%}

## The real question behind the comparison

Teams rarely compare AgentOps and Uncoil in the abstract. They compare them at a specific moment: right after the first multi-agent workflow starts producing outputs that a human has to eyeball, and right before someone asks "what's this costing us" in a planning meeting. Four groups tend to be in that room, and they're asking different questions:

**Engineers building the agent** want to know: what did the agent actually do on this run? Which tool call failed, and why? Did the LLM hallucinate a function argument, or did the tool itself return garbage? This is a debugging question, and it's the one AgentOps was built to answer with the least setup.

**Engineers responsible for keeping it working** want to know: how do we stop a bad prompt change or a bad model swap from silently degrading quality in production? Can we catch a regression before a customer does? This is an evaluation question, and it's the one that separates the two platforms most sharply.

**Whoever owns the budget** wants to know: what does this agent cost per request, per session, per customer? Which model or routing decision is driving spend? This is a cost-accounting question, and it's the one where "basic" and "automatic" stop meaning the same thing.

**Engineering leadership** wants to know: how much of our team's time goes into operating the observability tooling itself, versus building the product? This is a total-cost-of-ownership question, and it only becomes visible a few months in.

This article works through all four, with worked scenarios, real numbers, and — because neither platform is the objectively correct answer — the situations where AgentOps' simplicity is genuinely the better call.

## Core difference: recording fidelity vs. evaluation depth

{% highlight %}The choice between AgentOps and Uncoil is not about which one traces agents better. Both do. It's about whether your team's next bottleneck is understanding what an agent did, or proving what it did was good and knowing what it cost.{% /highlight %}

**AgentOps** is built for *teams that need to see agent behavior with near-zero instrumentation overhead*. You add a decorator or two, call `start_session()`, and the platform auto-detects agent framework patterns (CrewAI crews, AutoGen conversations, LangChain chains) and reconstructs a full session timeline without you designing a span hierarchy by hand. The upside: genuinely fast time-to-first-insight, and a replay experience purpose-built for multi-agent debugging. The downside: evaluation and cost analysis are thin. You get session data; you build (or bolt on) the judgment layer yourself.

**Uncoil** is built for *teams that need the judgment layer attached to the trace, not built separately*. Tracing, evaluation, cost tracking, and experimentation live in one product, over the same span data. The upside: you go from "we have traces" to "we have a quality gate and a cost breakdown" without a second integration project. The downside: instrumentation is a native SDK with explicit spans rather than a wrap-and-forget decorator, so first setup takes a little longer than AgentOps' fastest path, and you're paying for evaluation infrastructure whether or not you're using it yet.

## The architecture comparison

### AgentOps: decorator auto-instrumentation, replay-first

AgentOps' instrumentation model is deliberately low-friction:

- **Decorator and session wrapper based.** `agentops.init(api_key)` plus `agentops.start_session()` at the top of your run, and `@agentops.record_action(...)` around the functions you want tracked. No manual span tree to design.
- **Framework-aware auto-instrumentation.** CrewAI, AutoGen, and LangChain agent patterns are recognized automatically — crew handoffs, tool selection, and conversation turns show up in the session without extra code.
- **Session replay as the core product.** The dashboard reconstructs the full sequence of actions: which tool was called, with what arguments, what it returned, which LLM call followed, and where an error broke the chain. This is built specifically for the "what did my agent actually do" question that generic APM tools answer badly.
- **Python-first.** The SDK and framework integrations are strongest in the Python agent ecosystem, which is where most multi-agent frameworks (CrewAI, AutoGen, LangGraph-adjacent stacks) live.
- **Evaluation and cost are secondary surfaces.** Cost visibility exists at the session level (token counts, rough spend), but there is no native test-case library, no LLM-as-judge evaluator, and no batch regression runner comparable to a dedicated evaluation platform. Teams that need that layer typically integrate an external tool or build one.

The trade-off: minimal setup friction and best-in-class replay, in exchange for building your evaluation and detailed cost-accounting layer separately.

### Uncoil: native SDK, evaluation and cost attached natively

Uncoil's architecture keeps tracing, evaluation, and cost in one data model:

- **Explicit span-based SDK.** `uncoil.init({ apiKey })`, then `session.span({ name })` / `span.end({ output })` around the units of work you want visible. Slightly more code than a pure decorator, but you control exactly what's captured and how it's named.
- **Automatic cost calculation.** Every span with a model call is priced automatically using live provider pricing (OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, and others) — no separate pricing table to maintain.
- **Native evaluation framework.** Test cases live in the dashboard: exact match, substring, semantic similarity, and LLM-as-judge evaluators, run individually or in batch against historical traces. Regression alerts fire when a metric crosses a threshold.
- **Native multi-turn sessions.** Conversations spanning multiple LLM calls roll up cost and quality at the session level, not just the individual span level.
- **Experiment framework.** Compare prompt versions, model choices, or routing logic with statistical comparison built in, rather than manual trace-by-trace review.
- **TypeScript-first, broad integrations.** LangChain, LlamaIndex, and native OpenAI/Anthropic/Mistral instrumentation, with framework support that spans both TS and Python agent stacks.

The trade-off: you're paying for and setting up an evaluation and cost layer even in the early weeks when you might not need it yet, and the "wrap and forget" simplicity of a pure decorator is traded for a few more lines of deliberate instrumentation.

## Scenario 1: an agent system that needs cost optimization at scale

Your team runs a production agent that routes customer requests across Claude, GPT-4, and a smaller open model depending on complexity, then takes real actions (refunds, ticket updates, order lookups). You're eight weeks past initial launch. Three things are now true: quality regressions from prompt or model changes are hitting real customers before anyone notices; nobody can say with confidence what a single resolved ticket costs; and leadership wants a cost-per-resolution number for the next board update.

### With AgentOps

**Week 1: instrumentation.** Decorators go in fast — a day, maybe less, since the routing logic is already a Python service and CrewAI-style orchestration is auto-detected.

```python
# Illustrative: AgentOps decorator instrumentation
import agentops

agentops.init(api_key=os.environ["AGENTOPS_API_KEY"])

@agentops.record_action("route-and-execute")
def route_and_execute(user_message: str):
    session = agentops.start_session()
    complexity = analyze_complexity(user_message)
    selected_model = "gpt-4" if complexity > 0.7 else "claude-3"

    response = call_llm(selected_model, user_message)
    # Token counts are visible in the session; cost math is on you
    session.end_session(end_state="Success")
    return response
```

**Week 1-2: you can see everything.** Session replay shows exactly which requests routed where, and where a tool call returned something unexpected. This is genuinely valuable for the immediate incident review.

**Week 2 onward: the gap opens.** There is no native way to define "a refund response must not promise a dollar amount that doesn't match the order total" as a reusable, versioned test case and run it in batch against the last 5,000 sessions. That gets built by hand: pull sessions via the API, write your own scoring functions (likely another LLM call acting as judge), store results somewhere, and build a dashboard on top. Cost-per-resolution is a similar story — token counts exist, but turning them into "$0.34 per resolved ticket, broken out by model" means writing and maintaining your own pricing table against three providers' rate cards, which change.

**Result by week 8:** Excellent session replay and fast incident debugging from day one. Regression detection and true cost-per-resolution are still partially hand-built, and someone owns maintaining that glue code going forward.

### With Uncoil

**Day 1: instrumentation and first evaluated trace.**

```typescript
// Illustrative: Uncoil instrumentation
import { Uncoil } from "@uncoil/sdk";

const uncoil = new Uncoil({ apiKey: process.env.UNCOIL_API_KEY });

async function routeAndExecute(userMessage: string) {
  const session = uncoil.session({ userId: user.id, sessionId });

  const routingSpan = session.span({ name: "routing-decision" });
  const complexity = await analyzeComplexity(userMessage);
  const selectedModel = complexity > 0.7 ? "gpt-4" : "claude-3";
  routingSpan.end({ output: selectedModel });

  const llmSpan = session.span({ name: "llm-call", input: { model: selectedModel } });
  const response = await callLLM(selectedModel, userMessage);
  llmSpan.end({ output: response }); // cost is calculated automatically
  return response;
}
```

**Day 1-2: define the quality gate.** In the dashboard: "a refund response must not state a dollar amount that differs from `order.total`," scored with a mix of exact-match on the order ID and LLM-as-judge on the phrasing. Batch-run it against the existing trace history immediately.

**Day 2-3: cost is already broken out.** Per-call cost by model and provider is automatic; the session rollup gives cost-per-resolution without a manual pricing table. Set a threshold alert: "flag any session over $0.60."

**Result by day 3:** Tracing, a running quality gate, and a real cost-per-resolution figure, all from the same instrumentation pass. The team spends the following weeks tuning routing rules against real metrics instead of building the metrics pipeline itself.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Where the gap compounds" title="AgentOps gives you the incident review. Uncoil gives you the thing that prevents the incident." body="Both platforms would have shown you the session where the refund bot misquoted an order total. The difference shows up in what happens next. On AgentOps, that failure mode gets caught the next time a human happens to review a replay, unless you build a batch scorer yourself. On Uncoil, it's a test case that runs against every new session automatically, and the team gets an alert before the tenth customer hits it, not after. {% highlight %}Session replay answers 'what happened on this one run.' A native evaluation framework answers 'is this getting worse, across every run, without anyone watching.'{% /highlight %} Those are different jobs, and only one of them scales past the first incident review." tone="yellow" /%}

## Scenario 2: a team that needs fast setup and time-to-first-insight above all else

You're three engineers, five weeks into building an agentic research assistant on CrewAI. You don't have production traffic yet, you don't have a customer-facing quality bar to defend, and your only goal this sprint is understanding why the agent sometimes loops on a tool call instead of finishing the task. You need answers today, not a quality framework for a product that doesn't exist yet.

### With AgentOps

**Minutes 0-10:** `pip install agentops`, add `agentops.init()` and wrap the CrewAI kickoff. Because CrewAI is auto-instrumented, you don't touch the agent internals at all.

**Minute 10:** Run the agent. Open the dashboard. See the full session: which agent in the crew picked which tool, what arguments it passed, and — there it is — the search tool returning a malformed query on retry three, which is why the agent loops instead of completing. Fixed in the next fifteen minutes.

**Result:** From zero to root cause in under thirty minutes, with instrumentation you'll basically never think about again for pure debugging purposes.

### With Uncoil

**Minutes 0-20:** Instrumentation is a similar amount of code, but you're writing explicit spans around the CrewAI kickoff and tool calls rather than relying on framework auto-detection, since the emphasis is on span design that will still make sense once you add evaluation later.

**Minute 20-30:** You get the same visibility into the loop — the trace shows the same malformed retry — plus cost per run and the option to write a test case for "tool call must not repeat with an unchanged input," which you don't need yet but could use in week six.

**Result:** Same root cause found, roughly ten to twenty minutes slower to first insight, with evaluation and cost infrastructure sitting there unused until you need it.

**This scenario is the honest case for AgentOps.** When the job is purely "show me what the agent did, as fast as possible, with nothing else to configure," AgentOps' decorator model and CrewAI/AutoGen-native replay are a better fit than a platform that's optimized for the production-quality-gate problem you don't have yet.

## Deep comparison: feature by feature

![Bar chart scoring both platforms 0-10 across five categories: AgentOps leads setup speed (9 vs 6) and replay fidelity (10 vs 6), while Uncoil leads evaluation depth (9 vs 2), cost tracking (9 vs 3), and experimentation (8 vs 1)](/media/agentops-vs-uncoil-feature-radar.svg)

### Instrumentation

**AgentOps:**
- Decorator/wrapper-based; minimal code to get full session capture.
- Deep, opinionated auto-instrumentation for CrewAI, AutoGen, and LangChain — agent handoffs and tool selection appear without manual span design.
- Python-first SDK; this is where the framework coverage is strongest.
- Little control over span naming/structure — you get what the auto-instrumentation decides to capture, which is usually right for debugging but less flexible for custom evaluation criteria later.

**Uncoil:**
- Native SDK with explicit spans; a bit more code, full control over what's named and captured.
- Framework integrations for LangChain, LlamaIndex, and native OpenAI/Anthropic/Mistral instrumentation.
- TypeScript-first with solid Python support; broader language coverage for mixed-stack teams.
- Span structure is deliberately designed to also carry evaluation and cost metadata, which pays off once you need those layers.

**Winner:** AgentOps for raw setup speed and CrewAI/AutoGen-specific fidelity. Uncoil for span design that scales into evaluation without rework. If your agent framework is CrewAI or AutoGen and your only need today is visibility, AgentOps' auto-instrumentation is hard to beat on friction alone.

### Evaluation

**AgentOps:**
- No native test-case library comparable to a dedicated evaluation platform; quality review is largely manual, done by browsing session replays.
- Newer add-on evaluation surfaces exist but are not the product's center of gravity — the platform's identity is recording and replay, not judgment.
- No built-in regression detection or batch scoring against historical sessions; you build or bolt this on.

**Uncoil:**
- Native test-case library: define expected behavior once, version it, reuse it across evaluation runs.
- Batch evaluation runner against historical traces, with exact-match, substring, semantic-similarity, and LLM-as-judge scorers built in.
- Regression alerts when a metric crosses a threshold — the thing that catches a bad prompt change before a human does.
- Experiment framework for comparing prompt/model/routing variants with statistical comparison, not manual eyeballing.

**Winner:** Uncoil, decisively. {% highlight %}This is the single largest capability gap between the two platforms. If evaluation is a near-term requirement — not a someday requirement — AgentOps' current surface area does not close that gap without meaningful engineering investment on your side.{% /highlight %}

### Cost tracking

**AgentOps:**
- Session-level token counts and rough spend visibility.
- No automatic, always-current provider pricing engine comparable to Uncoil's; turning tokens into an accurate, provider-current dollar figure is manual work you maintain.
- Good enough for "is this session obviously expensive," not precise enough for a board-ready cost-per-resolution number without extra work.

**Uncoil:**
- Automatic per-call cost calculated from live provider pricing (OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, and others) — no pricing table to maintain yourself.
- Cost rolls up per session, per agent, and trends over time, out of the box.
- Threshold alerts ("flag sessions over $0.60") and optimization suggestions tied to routing/model choice.

**Winner:** Uncoil. Cost accountability is a business requirement the moment finance or a founder asks for it, and AgentOps' basic visibility means you're building the pricing layer yourself right when you least want to.

### Session management

**AgentOps:**
- This is the category AgentOps is strongest in. Sessions are the native unit of the product, replayed step-by-step with full framework context (which crew member did what, in what order).
- Best-in-class for the specific job of "reconstruct exactly what happened on this run."

**Uncoil:**
- Native multi-turn sessions with cost and quality metrics rolled up at the session level, but the emphasis is on session-level metrics over session-level replay fidelity.
- Trace timeline views are solid for debugging but not purpose-built for the same step-by-step replay experience AgentOps offers.

**Winner:** AgentOps for pure replay fidelity. Uncoil for session-level cost and quality metrics. If your team's primary use of "session" is watching it back to debug, AgentOps wins this category outright.

### Deployment and operations

**AgentOps:**
- SaaS, hosted dashboard. No infrastructure to run.
- Setup is close to zero-ops: install, init, done.

**Uncoil:**
- SaaS, hosted dashboard. No infrastructure to run.
- Slightly more setup investment upfront (explicit span design), but equally zero-ops after that — no infrastructure maintenance on either side.

**Winner:** Tie on operations. Both are managed SaaS with no self-hosting story; neither asks your team to run infrastructure. The difference is entirely in setup time and what's included once you're live, not in who operates what.

## Operational and organizational trade-offs

Beyond the feature checklist, the two platforms create different organizational patterns over time.

**With AgentOps**, the team that owns instrumentation tends to stay small and stays close to the agent code — usually the same engineers who wrote the CrewAI crew are the ones reading the replays. That's efficient early on. But as soon as a second team (QA, a data/eval specialist, or a non-engineering stakeholder like a support lead) needs to define what "good" looks like, they hit a wall: there's no native place to encode "a good response looks like X" that isn't a person reading a replay and forming an opinion. That knowledge stays tribal, in engineers' heads, rather than becoming a reusable, versioned artifact.

**With Uncoil**, the test-case library becomes a shared artifact almost immediately — a support lead or product manager can define "what good looks like" as a test case without touching the agent code, and engineers can see the same score history everyone else sees. This distributes the judgment work away from whoever happens to be reading replays that day, at the cost of someone needing to actually write and maintain those test cases, which is real work too.

**Team size matters more than it first appears.** A team of two or three, moving fast pre-launch, rarely benefits from a shared test-case library — there's no one else to share it with yet, and the overhead of writing test cases for a product that's still changing weekly is often wasted effort. A team of ten or more, with a support function and a product owner who both have opinions about output quality, benefits enormously from moving that judgment out of engineers' heads and into a shared, versioned system.

> **A practical signal:** if you've caught yourself writing "can someone check if this response is actually good" in a Slack thread more than once this month, that's the moment a native evaluation framework starts paying for itself. Before that point, session replay alone is often enough.

