{% highlight %}At production scale, the numbers flip. Uncoil is roughly 2.1x cheaper annually, and reaches a working quality gate in days rather than the 6-10 weeks it takes to hand-build equivalent evaluation and cost-tracking infrastructure on top of AgentOps. The crossover isn't about tracing cost — it's entirely about who builds and maintains the evaluation and pricing layer.{% /highlight %}

> **The crossover point isn't a fixed call volume.** It's the moment your team needs a repeatable, automated answer to "did this get worse" or "what did this cost," instead of a person reading a replay and forming an opinion. For some teams that's month one; for others it's month eight. Track when that question starts coming up in planning meetings — that's your signal, not a specific traffic number.

## Trade-offs and honest boundaries

### Choose AgentOps when:

**1. Your only near-term job is understanding agent behavior, fast.**
- You're pre-launch, iterating on agent logic daily, and the main question is "why did it do that," not "is it getting worse."
- **Mitigation if you outgrow this:** the migration to a platform with native evaluation is straightforward once you actually need it — see below.

**2. Your stack is CrewAI or AutoGen and framework-native replay matters.**
- AgentOps' auto-instrumentation and replay UI are purpose-built for exactly these multi-agent frameworks, and the fidelity shows.
- **Mitigation:** if you later need evaluation, you can run both platforms in parallel rather than choosing one exclusively — more on this below.

**3. You genuinely don't have engineering time to spend on a heavier SDK integration.**
- A two-or-three-person team mid-sprint often can't justify twenty minutes of deliberate span design when ten minutes of decorator wrapping answers today's question.
- **Mitigation:** revisit this decision the moment a second person outside engineering needs to weigh in on output quality.

**4. Budget is the binding constraint pre-revenue.**
- At early-stage scale, AgentOps' pricing is meaningfully lower, and you're not paying for evaluation/cost infrastructure you can't use yet.
- **Mitigation:** budget for a platform switch (or an additional platform) once cost-per-resolution or quality regressions become a real conversation, not a hypothetical one.

### Choose Uncoil when:

**1. Production quality gates are a near-term requirement, not a someday one.**
- {% highlight %}If you can already name the failure mode you're worried about ("the refund bot might misquote a dollar amount"), that's a test case waiting to be written, and only one of these platforms has a place to write it.{% /highlight %}
- **Mitigation:** none needed — this is squarely Uncoil's use case.

**2. Cost accountability is a business requirement, not a nice-to-have.**
- Finance or leadership wants per-request or per-resolution cost, broken out by model, on an ongoing basis.
- **Mitigation:** Uncoil's automatic pricing removes the maintenance burden of a hand-built pricing table; this is hard to replicate cheaply on AgentOps.

**3. Your team spans multiple stakeholders who need to define "good."**
- Support, product, and engineering all have opinions about output quality, and you need a shared, versioned place to encode that.
- **Mitigation:** the test-case library is exactly this shared artifact; invest the setup time once and stakeholders self-serve after.

**4. You're running multiple models or providers and need to compare them.**
- The experiment framework gives statistical comparison across prompt/model/routing variants instead of manual trace review.
- **Mitigation:** none needed — this is a native capability, not a workaround.

### Hybrid approach: AgentOps for debugging, Uncoil for production gates

Some teams genuinely run both, and it's a reasonable pattern rather than a compromise:

- **Upside:** AgentOps' replay stays in the loop for day-to-day debugging during active development, where its fidelity and near-zero setup shine. Uncoil handles the production-facing quality gate and cost reporting, where its evaluation and pricing depth matter most.
- **Downside:** two integrations to maintain, and no single source of truth — a session that looks fine in AgentOps replay might still fail an Uncoil test case, and someone has to know to check both.
- **When to use it:** teams actively building new agent capabilities (where AgentOps' debugging loop stays valuable) while simultaneously running a stable production surface that needs quality gates (where Uncoil's evaluation is required). This is common in the months right around a launch, when both jobs are happening at once.

> **Don't run both indefinitely "just in case."** Two platforms mean two bills, two integrations to keep current, and a real risk that "we have observability" quietly becomes "half our team checks AgentOps and half checks Uncoil, and nobody checks both." Set a review date — 60-90 days after launch is reasonable — and consolidate onto whichever one is actually answering your team's current question.

## Decision framework for practitioners and leaders

### For technical practitioners

| Question | Score if yes | Suggests |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Is your framework CrewAI or AutoGen, and is replay fidelity the priority? | +5 AgentOps | Native framework support is a real advantage |
| Do you need a batch-run, versioned test case today (not eventually)? | +5 Uncoil | Evaluation is a near-term requirement |
| Is your team under 5 engineers, pre-revenue? | +3 AgentOps | Budget and setup speed matter more than depth |
| Does finance or leadership want per-request cost this quarter? | +4 Uncoil | Automatic cost calculation removes a real build |
| Do multiple non-engineering stakeholders need to define "good"? | +4 Uncoil | A shared test-case library is the right tool |
| Is your only current question "what did the agent just do"? | +4 AgentOps | Session replay alone answers this |
| Do you need statistical comparison across prompt/model variants? | +4 Uncoil | Native experiment framework, not manual review |

**Scoring:** If AgentOps score > 10, prioritize fast setup and replay fidelity — start there. If Uncoil score > 12, evaluation and cost tracking are your real constraint — the setup-time difference won't matter once you're past week two. If both are high, the hybrid approach above is a reasonable interim step.

### For engineering and product leaders

**Time-to-first-insight:** Do you need an answer today or can you invest half a day?
- **Today, no room for setup:** AgentOps. The decorator model wins on raw speed.
- **Half a day is fine, and you want the answer to also carry a cost and a pass/fail:** Uncoil.

**Where is the team in its lifecycle?**
- **Pre-launch, iterating daily:** AgentOps, unless a quality gate is already a known near-term requirement.
- **Post-launch, defending a quality bar:** Uncoil. Hand-building evaluation after launch is a much worse position than having it from day one.

**Who needs to weigh in on quality?**
- **Just engineers, for now:** AgentOps' replay is enough.
- **Support, product, or leadership too:** Uncoil's shared test-case library removes the bottleneck of "someone has to read the replay and decide."

**What does the budget conversation look like?**
- **Every dollar matters pre-revenue:** AgentOps is meaningfully cheaper at this stage — use the savings.
- **Cost-per-resolution is already a board topic:** Uncoil's automatic pricing pays for itself against the FTE cost of building it yourself.

## Migration paths and reversibility

### From AgentOps to Uncoil (low-risk, common)

This is the more common direction, since it typically happens as a natural response to hitting a real evaluation or cost-reporting need rather than dissatisfaction with AgentOps itself.

1. **Parallel instrumentation (weeks 1-2):** Add the Uncoil SDK alongside AgentOps rather than ripping AgentOps out. Both can observe the same agent runs simultaneously with minimal conflict, since they're independent instrumentation layers.
2. **Define your first test cases (week 1-2):** Start with the failure modes you already know about from AgentOps replays — these translate directly into Uncoil test cases, since you've already done the hard part of identifying what "bad" looks like.
3. **Validate cost figures (week 2):** Cross-check Uncoil's automatic cost calculation against your own AgentOps-based estimates to build confidence in the numbers before they go in front of finance.
4. **Decide, per use case (week 2-4):** Many teams keep AgentOps in the loop for active development debugging and let Uncoil own the production quality gate and cost reporting — this is the hybrid pattern above, and it's a perfectly reasonable landing point rather than a stepping stone.

**Total migration risk:** Low. AgentOps and Uncoil don't compete for the same instrumentation hooks in most stacks, so this is closer to "add a capability" than "rip and replace."

### From Uncoil to AgentOps (rare, and worth questioning)

Teams occasionally move this direction when evaluation and cost tracking turn out to be less of a near-term need than expected, and setup simplicity becomes the priority again — for instance, after a pivot back to an early-stage exploration phase.

1. **Confirm the need is real, not just budget pressure (before starting):** If the actual issue is cost, consider whether a lighter Uncoil tier solves it before doing a full platform switch — evaluation you've already built out has real value that's expensive to rebuild later.
2. **Export what you can (week 1):** Trace history and evaluation results specific to Uncoil's data model don't map directly onto AgentOps' session-replay model; expect to lose the structured pass/fail history, not just reformat it.
3. **Re-instrument (weeks 1-2):** Swap explicit spans for AgentOps decorators; this is usually less code, not more.
4. **Accept the evaluation gap consciously (ongoing):** Whatever quality gates existed in Uncoil don't have a direct equivalent on AgentOps; either rebuild them externally or accept a return to manual review.

**Total migration risk:** Moderate. The instrumentation swap itself is easy; the real cost is losing a working evaluation layer and needing to consciously decide what replaces it, if anything.

> **Decision rule:** Choose the platform that matches the failure you need to prevent next: AgentOps for fast replay-led debugging, or Uncoil when evaluation and cost accountability must operate across runs.

## Action checklist for decision-makers

- [ ] **Stage check:** Are you pre-launch and iterating on agent behavior daily, or post-launch defending a quality bar? This alone answers most of the AgentOps-vs-Uncoil question.
- [ ] **Framework check:** If you're on CrewAI or AutoGen and replay fidelity is your top need, weight AgentOps higher regardless of stage.
- [ ] **Stakeholder check:** List who needs to judge output quality. If it's more than "the engineers who wrote the agent," a shared test-case library (Uncoil) is worth the setup time.
- [ ] **Cost-reporting check:** Has finance or leadership asked for per-request or per-resolution cost yet? If yes, that's a near-term Uncoil requirement, not a someday one.
- [ ] **Budget check:** Run the TCO math for your actual scale using the worked scenarios above as a template, not as your answer — plug in your own call volume and team size.
- [ ] **Prototype:** If undecided, instrument a single agent flow with both SDKs in parallel for a week. They don't conflict, and a week is enough to feel the difference in setup time and daily usefulness.
- [ ] **Reversibility:** Note that AgentOps-to-Uncoil is low-risk and common; Uncoil-to-AgentOps means consciously giving up a working evaluation layer. Weight your initial choice accordingly if you're unsure.
- [ ] **Review date:** Set a 60-90 day checkpoint post-launch to confirm the platform still matches your current bottleneck, not the one you had when you chose it.

If your near-term bottleneck is production confidence and cost accountability, the [complete guide to AI agent observability](/blog/ai-agent-observability-complete-guide) walks through how tracing, evaluation, and cost tracking fit together as one system. If evaluation itself is the open question — what to test, how to score it, when to gate a deploy on it — the [practical guide to AI agent evaluation](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) is the deeper resource on building that layer well, whichever platform you land on.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Does AgentOps have any evaluation capabilities at all?" answer="Some, but they are not the center of the product. AgentOps' core strength is auto-instrumented session recording and replay; evaluation exists mostly as manual review of that replay, plus newer add-on tooling that is not comparable in depth to a dedicated evaluation platform's native test-case library, batch runner, and regression detection. If your near-term need is a repeatable, automated answer to 'did this get worse,' plan to either build that layer yourself on top of AgentOps' session data or pair AgentOps with a platform like Uncoil that has evaluation as a first-class feature." /%}

{% faq-item question="Is AgentOps actually faster to set up than Uncoil?" answer="For the specific job of 'see what my agent did,' yes, usually — a decorator and a couple of init calls get you a replayable session in around ten minutes, especially on CrewAI or AutoGen where framework auto-detection does the span design for you. Uncoil's native SDK takes a bit longer, typically twenty to thirty minutes, because you're writing explicit spans that are also structured to carry evaluation and cost metadata later. The gap is real but small in absolute terms — it matters most when you truly have nothing beyond the debugging question to answer today." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can I use AgentOps and Uncoil together?" answer="Yes, and it's a reasonable pattern rather than a workaround. They instrument independently and don't conflict, so many teams keep AgentOps in the loop for day-to-day agent debugging during active development while using Uncoil for the production-facing quality gate and cost reporting. The main cost is running two integrations and two bills, plus the discipline to avoid a split-brain situation where different people check different dashboards. Set a review date to consolidate once one job clearly dominates your team's actual day-to-day use." /%}

{% faq-item question="How does cost tracking actually differ between the two platforms?" answer="AgentOps surfaces token counts and rough spend at the session level, which is enough to notice an obviously expensive run but not precise enough for a board-ready cost-per-resolution figure without extra work — you'd maintain your own provider pricing table across OpenAI, Anthropic, and others, and keep it current as rates change. Uncoil calculates cost automatically per call using live provider pricing, rolls it up per session and per agent, and supports threshold alerts out of the box. If cost accountability is a near-term requirement rather than a someday one, this gap is one of the more expensive things to build yourself." /%}

{% faq-item question="When would I genuinely prefer AgentOps over Uncoil?" answer="Choose AgentOps when your near-term job is understanding agent behavior with the least possible setup friction — you're pre-launch, iterating on agent logic daily, running CrewAI or AutoGen where its auto-instrumentation and replay fidelity are strongest, and you don't yet have a customer-facing quality bar or a cost-per-resolution number to defend. At that stage, AgentOps is also meaningfully cheaper, since you're not paying for evaluation and cost infrastructure you can't use yet. The moment a second stakeholder outside engineering needs to define 'good,' or finance asks for real cost numbers, or a bad prompt change needs to be caught automatically rather than noticed by a human reading a replay, that calculus flips toward a platform with evaluation and cost tracking built in natively." /%}
